  "notes": [
   "Parts are the same as in step 1; none added or dropped.",
   "The schematic image was not visible, so the wiring follows the sketch's pin defines: pin 5 is the white LED (meeting room), pin 2 is RGB red, pin 3 is RGB blue and pin 4 is RGB green (lounge area).",
   "The MKR ENV Shield (new type, because lookup_part found nothing for it) stacks on the MKR WiFi 1010. Its D2-D5 and GND headers pass straight through to the same pins on the board, so I added joined and electrical edges for those pins. The jumper wires plug into the shield's headers.",
   "Each LED channel has its own 220 ohm resistor, between the LED leg and the Arduino wire. The resistor sits in the same breadboard column as the LED leg, with the other end across the centre gap in the same column on the lower half. The jumper wire to the board goes in the lower-half hole next to it.",
   "The RGB LED is common cathode, so COM goes to ground. The white LED's anode goes through its resistor to pin 5 and its cathode to ground. The ground rail is tn.1 and the GND jumper (wire5) feeds it.",
   "The breadboard holes used (e.g. 10t.a, 20b.g) are my own layout choice. Only the connectivity is taken from the tutorial.",
   "The resistor count (4) and jumper wire count (7) are assumptions, because the parts list gives no quantities.",
   "The ENV shield's temperature sensor is read over its own I2C connection to the MKR board. That connection is internal to the stack and is not drawn as a separate edge."
  ]
 },
